  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 227 AM EST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\n- Rain Favored Thanksgiving Afternoon into Friday\n\nNot much has changed with regard to the late week system. Ensemble\nguidance has continued to indicate that Michigan will primarily be\nimpacted by rain especially later Thanksgiving afternoon into\nFriday with some wet snow mixing in at times. Ensemble mean and\ndeterministic guidance for the 500mb upper low path continues to\nindicate an Ohio Valley track with a favorable 850mb low track\nfrom near Indianapolis 00z Friday to Detroit by 12 Friday.\nAn atypical 70-80kt LLJ setup with 850mb air trajectories off the\nAtlantic Ocean will be moving in late on Thanksgiving. Rain is\nvery likely for southern Lower Michigan as early as Thanksgiving\nafternoon near I-94 and moving further north later in the evening.\n\n00z ECWMF soundings for GRR and LAN indicate that rain is the\nfavored p-type with some wet snow mixing in at times. While the\nDGZ will be saturated along with lift, it will be located way up\nin the 15k-20k ft layer with massive riming located below it and\nsurface wet bulb temps only getting down to 2C. There should be a\nmelting layer Thanksgiving afternoon/evening that forces any \nfrozen precip to melt on its way down to earth, with some\npossibility for nighttime thermal profiles supportive of wet snow\nmixing in with the rain, also lasting into Friday morning. So,\nwhile a light slushy coating of snow on unpaved surfaces could \noccur across portions of the region by Friday morning, the most \nlikely scenario is that rain dominates this event with some wet\nsnow mixing in and melting on contact. In general, rainfall totals\nshould range from 0.10\"-0.25\" across central Lower MI to\n0.25\"-0.75\" everywhere else with some totals close to 1.00\"\nespecially near and east of I-69.\n\n- Chance for Rain Showers or Rain/Snow Mix Over Weekend\n\nUpper troughing pattern impacts the region for the weekend.\nEnsemble guidance supports mainly rain with perhaps a rain/snow\nmix at times. The deterministic ECMWF and GEM develop an area of\nlow pressure on Sunday that may bring some steadier rain showers\nto the area.\n\n- No Arctic Air in Sight\n\nWith the very strong El Nino in place, it will continue to be very\ndifficult to bring any Arctic air down into the U.S. In truth, the\nArctic air right now is much warmer than normal for late November.\nAn analysis of surface temperature anomalies since October \nindicate substantially above normal readings across Canada and \ninto the Arctic reaches. In fact, this month alone has been 15F \nabove normal across the Northwest Territories into the Arctic \nCircle. The ECE weeklies guidance that goes through the end of\nDecember shows no abatement of this type of pattern, with surface\ntemperature anomalies potentially reaching an astounding 15F-20F \nabove normal during December across the northern provinces into \nHudson Bay.\n\nMean upper heights indicate a dominant zonal pattern into\nDecember across the northern U.S. as opposed to an amplified \nridge west/trough east pattern. These two factors, assuming long\nrange guidance is on the right track, indicate reduced\nopportunities for snow and cold. Snow events may need to come from\nphasing opportunities with southern U.S. wave generation, such as\nwhat is going to occur over Thanksgiving, but with colder\ntemperatures. We will see how model guidance trends as we head\ninto December.",
